# CODEOWNERS — whom GitHub requests for review when a matching path changes.
#
# There is one maintainer today, so every line points to the same handle. The value
# right now is the automatic review request and a written record of who answers for
# what; as the project grows, the per-path lines are where a module's owner is named
# without handing them the whole tree. Last matching pattern wins, so the specific
# blocks below override the default.
#
# NOTE: this file only *assigns* reviewers — it does not *require* them. Requiring
# code-owner approval is a separate ruleset switch, and turning it on while there is a
# single owner would lock that owner out of merging their own PRs. Leave it off until a
# second maintainer exists.

# Default owner for everything not matched below.
*                       @haed

# Governance, licensing and the security policy answer for the project as a whole, not
# for one module, and stay with the maintainer even once module owners exist. Listed
# explicitly so a later, narrower rule never quietly takes them.
/LICENSE                @haed
/NOTICE                 @haed
/SECURITY.md            @haed
/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md     @haed
/CONTRIBUTING.md        @haed
/TRADEMARKS.md          @haed
/NAMESPACE.md           @haed

# CI, release plumbing and this file itself — the keys to the kingdom. A change here can
# weaken every other guard, so it stays with the maintainer regardless of who owns the
# code being built.
/.github/               @haed

# Security-critical code. Auth is the last module to hand over — a wrong answer here is
# someone reading data that was meant to stay private (see SECURITY.md). Kept explicit so
# handing it over is a deliberate act, not a default.
/sempods-auth/          @haed
/sempods-auth-core/     @haed
